 }
diff --git a/Servex/ext/remote/doc/voice-logging.md b/Servex/ext/remote/doc/voice-logging.md
index 9b5d75ddb..a4c06f734 100644
--- a/Servex/ext/remote/doc/voice-logging.md
+++ b/Servex/ext/remote/doc/voice-logging.md
@@ -26,4 +26,4 @@ Work in a worktree. Do not touch `Servex/agents/Sessions.js`, `ux/Dictate/chat.j
 
 Every tool call from the voice app runs `RemoteMCP.voice_session(sid)`, which also calls `point_caller(id)`: it sets the `voice-app` registry row's `session_id` to that record's id (`External.register`, which changes nothing else in the row). `Agents.session_of("voice-app")` reads it, so each agent the voice app spawns afterwards gets that record as its `parent_session`. Only the registry row is set: the voice app is never a live agent, so there is no live entry to update. Before this, the row kept the id it was registered with on 2026-10-19 and tonight's children hung under an October session. Test: the "children of the voice app" block in `RemoteMCP.test.mjs`. Needs a Servex restart.
 
-**A second cloud voice session on the same day (current behaviour, written down, not changed).** The record's id comes from the connection's `register_session` title (`cloud-voice-<title>`), else `cloud-voice-app-<YYYYMMDD>`. So two calls on one day that never name themselves share one record, and a titled call gets its own. The registry row holds one id, the one opened by the most recent call, so two live conversations alternating calls would flip it. Proposal (not built): give an untitled connection `cloud-voice-app-<YYYYMMDD>-<n>` from its MCP session id, and key the parent by connection (the spawn's `sid`) instead of the single registry row. Alternative: keep one record per day and make same-day conversations tabs inside it; cheaper, but costs and children of two conversations stay mixed.
+**One record per conversation, not per date (built 2026-11-07).** The Claude app opens a new MCP connection every few calls (2,288 connections in `remote.sessions.json`, about 150 on 11-07 alone), so a connection is not a conversation and the server never sees a conversation id. `RemoteMCP.voice_session` therefore keeps the caller's record in use under `remote.sessions.json` key `caller:voice-app` (`RemoteMCP.CURRENT`): a call on a connection with no title of its own stays on that record while calls come less than 2 hours apart (`RemoteMCP.IDLE_MS`), past local midnight too; a `register_session` title, or a longer gap, opens another (`cloud-voice-<title>`, else `cloud-voice-app-<YYYYMMDD>`). An id the Sessions store already has is filed in its own folder (the day it began), never a second folder today. Before this, the 11-06 conversation went on at 00:13 local into a new `cloud-voice-app-20261107` record; the two are linked by a Session entry on the 11-06 record and a `continues` note on the 11-07 one. It was the only voice record that crossed midnight (gaps between voice records, 10-20 to 11-07: 64 min, then 206 min and up). **Still open:** two cloud chats at once share one record, because the server cannot tell them apart; the only exact key is one the agent passes itself (a title on every reconnect, or an id on every call), which the voice agent has not reliably done so far.
